<p>
Mediation is one of the most widely used tools in management and organizational research. It allows researchers to move beyond simple relationships (X → Y) and ask a more meaningful question: <i>how</i> and <i>why</i> does an effect occur? By introducing a mediator, researchers attempt to uncover the underlying mechanism linking predictors and outcomes.
</p>

<p>
In practice, mediation models almost always include covariates. These are additional variables—such as age, gender, or tenure—that researchers include to control for potential confounds and reduce unexplained variance. The conventional assumption is straightforward: adding covariates should improve statistical precision and, in turn, increase statistical power.
</p>

<p>
However, this assumption does not always hold. Covariates only help when they are meaningfully related to the variables in the model. When covariates are weakly related—or included by default rather than by design—they can actually reduce statistical power, making it harder to detect real effects.
</p>

<p>
More importantly, the role of covariates depends on the research design. In survey studies, where variables are continuous and not randomized, covariates that correlate with predictors can substantially affect power. In contrast, in experimental designs with randomized treatments, the treatment is independent of other variables, and the covariate’s relationship with the mediator becomes more relevant.
</p>

<p>
In my work, I use Monte Carlo simulations to systematically examine how covariates affect statistical power in mediation models. The simulations vary the relationship between covariates and key variables across different settings, including simple mediation, serial mediation, and moderated mediation. I also compare continuous and binary covariates, as their measurement properties can lead to different power outcomes.
</p>

<p>
The results challenge a common assumption in applied research: covariates do not universally improve statistical power. In some cases, their inclusion has little effect; in others, it can meaningfully reduce power. These findings suggest that covariates should be treated as a design decision rather than a default choice.
</p>

<p>
Overall, this work highlights the importance of aligning methodological decisions with research design. Rather than automatically including covariates, researchers should consider when and how they contribute to inference—and incorporate them into power analysis accordingly.
</p>
